    "content": "AI to ROI Reports & Data: The Future of Enterprise AI from PitchBook AI to ROI - By Ray Rike and Peter Buchanan Subscribe Sign in AI to ROI Reports & Data: The Future of Enterprise AI from PitchBook Two Connected Reports on the Future of Enterprise AI: SaaS Is Dead, Long Live SaS & Through the Looking Glass from PitchBook Ray Rike and Peter Buchanan Apr 07, 2026 Share The AI Software Revolution Meets SaaS Reinvention About PitchBook and the Authors PitchBook is a Morningstar company that provides data, research, and analysis for professionals working across private markets. Its Institutional Research Group publishes analyst notes covering venture capital, private equity, and emerging technology sectors. The authors are Rudy Torrijos, Director of Industry Research, and Derek Hernandez, Senior Research Analyst covering Enterprise SaaS and Infrastructure SaaS. The reports are a two-part series that delivers a connected argument about the future of enterprise software and AI. What the Reports Cover Report #1: SaaS Is Dead, Long Live SaS (February 9, 2026) The first report opens with a direct challenge to the SaaSpocalypse. PitchBook argues that: Investors panicked after Anthropic launched an AI legal services plugin, selling software positions amid the fear that generative AI models would entirely commoditize enterprise software. The real shift is not from software to AI, but from selling access to tools to selling completed work. The authors call this new model Service as Software , or SaS . Instead of charging per seat, vendors charge per outcome, per resolved ticket, per filed report, or per completed workflow. Outcome-based pricing allows software budgets to merge with payroll budgets, which PitchBook estimates represents an 8x revenue uplift per unit. The report identifies ten structural moats that protect incumbent software vendors: Distribution, data context, switching costs, workflow integration, security and compliance, AI as a revenue layer, outcome pricing, vendor consolidation, investment scale, and deep business logic. The report closes with specific playbooks for PE investors, VCs, public equity managers, SaaS founders, and IT leaders. Report #2: Through the Looking Glass (March 9, 2026) The second report moves from the strategic case to the operational mechanics of SaS (Service as Software) . It asks a harder question: What does it actually take for a vendor to deliver on the SaS promise? The answer centers on what PitchBook calls the autonomy gap. That’s the operational cost incurred every time an AI agent fails and requires a human to intervene. Until that gap closes, labor replacement stays out of reach. Closing it requires three things to happen simultaneously: Vendors must build agents that can operate reliably without constant human oversight Vendors and enterprises must work together to move to outcome-based pricing to capture the value of labor. Enterprises must stand up AgentOps functions to govern the digital workforce. To he",
